|~ What is declared under `::verb`, the kind of a verb: the refusals
   its head raises, of its declaration and of the slots a call fills. ~|

:verb/PayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ `::verb` is a tag over a quote, and a payload of another kind is
      refused.

      ~(
        ::verb[1]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::verb/PayloadNotQuoteError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/SlotWithoutBodyError
  |~~ The head of a verb is read before it runs, so a slot is declared
      by a kind, a set of kinds or a literal, and a leading declaration
      without a body is refused where the verb is made. A declaration
      whose body computes stands outside the head, the first step of the
      body.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:x |~~ How many. ~~| | add x)
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/SlotWithoutBodyError :x]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/RoleNotKindError
  |~~ The roles of a verb, `:subject` and `:returns`, are declared by a
      kind or a set of kinds, and `:returns` by `/` for the subject's
      own kind.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:returns 3 | add 1)
        !| [type /role]
        |  eq [::verb/RoleNotKindError :returns]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/SlotMissingError
  |~~ A slot declared by a kind alone is filled by the call, and a call
      that leaves it empty is refused by the name of the slot.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m
        !| [type /verbName /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/SlotMissingError :m :x]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/ModifiersBeyondSlotsError
  |~~ A verb takes as many modifiers as it has slots, one more when
      the first is its subject, unless `*` gathers the ones that
      remain.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m 1 2 3
        !| [type /slotCount /actualCount]
        |  eq [::verb/ModifiersBeyondSlotsError 1 3]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/SlotNotOfKindsError
  |~~ The subject of a verb and a slot declared by a set of kinds take
      a value of one of their kinds, reached through the tags the value
      carries, and refuse any other: no constructor turns the value in
      the pipe into the kind a verb serves, and no one constructor reads
      a value for a set of kinds. A slot of one kind reads its modifier
      through the kind's constructor.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:limit #[::number ::null] | limit) | 5 | m "a"
        !| [type /slot /kinds]
        |  eq [::verb/SlotNotOfKindsError :limit #[::null ::number]]
       )
      ~(
        :inc ::verb~(:subject ::number | add 1) | "a" | inc
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/SlotNotOfKindsError :subject]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/CodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ A slot of code takes a quote `~(…)` or a verb and nothing else,
      since a value is never read as code, nor the error a modifier
      answered when it ran at the call.

      ~(
        :run ::verb~(:code ::quote | apply code) | 5 | run 1
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/CodeNotQuoteError :code]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

